"""Editorial programme expansion based on the existing GECP catalogue.
Guides are explanatory content, not reports of delivered projects or promised services.
"""
PROFILES = {
'gccs': {
'audience': [('Independent learners', 'Build a learning project around a real question. Autodidacts’ Academy connects self-directed study with reflection, practical outputs and opportunities to learn with others.'), ('Returning learners', 'CELL explores complementary and lifelong learning routes for people returning to education or developing foundational capabilities alongside work and family commitments.'), ('Practitioners and mentors', 'Bring a practical challenge to a community of practice, share a demonstration or help learners connect an idea with its application.')],
'journey': [('Choose a learning question', 'Describe what you want to understand or do. A focused question makes it easier to identify suitable materials, people and practical exercises.'), ('Build a manageable plan', 'Map your available time, starting skills and preferred learning format. Discuss the support you need with the GCCS team.'), ('Make learning visible', 'Keep a learning journal, prototype, portfolio or demonstration. Use feedback to decide what to explore next.')],
'faq': [('Is GCCS a conventional school?', 'GCCS is presented as a complementary education and lifelong-learning platform. Enquire about the structure, facilitation and recognition of any specific activity before enrolling.'), ('Can I contribute knowledge as well as learn?', 'Yes, you can express interest in mentoring, guest teaching or a community of practice. The team will discuss the audience, learning purpose and delivery arrangements.')],
'blogs': [('learning-project', 'Turn a personal interest into a learning project', 'Independent learning', [('Start with something you want to do', 'A learning project becomes easier to sustain when it has a visible purpose. Instead of choosing a very broad subject, identify one task you want to complete or one question you want to answer. Write down why it matters and how you will recognise progress.'), ('Combine study with a small output', 'Read, observe and practise in short cycles. After each cycle, produce something you can explain: a diagram, a short reflection, a working example or a demonstration. The output helps reveal gaps that more reading alone may not expose.'), ('Ask for useful feedback', 'Show your work to someone who can ask questions about your reasoning. Record what changed after the conversation. Within the GCCS concept, independent learning and shared expertise complement each other; neither requires you to pretend you already know the answer.')]), ('community-practice', 'What makes a community of practice useful?', 'Peer learning', [('Begin with a shared problem', 'A community of practice needs more than a shared title. Participants benefit from a specific problem they recognise in their work or learning. A focused question gives the group a reason to exchange methods, examples and experience.'), ('Bring examples, not only opinions', 'A draft, a case description or an unfinished attempt makes discussion concrete. Explain the context and remove confidential details before sharing. Invite participants to describe what they tried and what remains uncertain.'), ('Keep a record of learning', 'End each discussion with a short account of the ideas worth testing. Agree who will try what and when the group will compare observations. This turns a conversation into a continuing learning process rather than a one-off meeting.')])]},
'pec': {
'audience': [('Learners choosing a next step', 'Explore interests, strengths and educational routes before committing to a pathway. PEC brings the discussion back to the learner’s goals and current circumstances.'), ('Families supporting decisions', 'Prepare questions about entry requirements, learning environments, costs and support needs. The aim is a shared understanding of options rather than a decision made on someone else’s behalf.'), ('Applicants navigating opportunities', 'Use SPSOW as a starting point for organising placement and scholarship information, then verify current requirements with the original institution.')],
'journey': [('Describe your starting point', 'Tell the team your education or career stage, interests and the decisions you are facing. Initial enquiries do not require confidential school records.'), ('Compare possible routes', 'Discuss learning requirements, time commitments and the evidence needed to assess each option. Record questions that require confirmation from an institution.'), ('Prepare a pathway plan', 'Translate the conversation into next actions: further research, an application checklist, preparation tasks or a follow-up discussion.')],
'faq': [('Does an enquiry guarantee admission or a scholarship?', 'No. Institutions and funding bodies make their own decisions. PEC supports pathway exploration and preparation; current requirements must be checked with the relevant provider.'), ('What should I include in the first enquiry?', 'Share your current stage, the decision you need help with and your preferred way to arrange a conversation. Do not send confidential records through the public enquiry form.')],
'blogs': [('pathway-questions', 'Five questions to bring to a pathway conversation', 'Career guidance', [('Clarify the decision', 'Write down the decision you need to make and when you need to make it. Ask what you hope the next step will make possible. A specific decision is easier to explore than a general request to find the best career.'), ('Explore fit and requirements', 'Ask which parts of a subject or activity hold your attention, what evidence you have of your strengths, and what the route requires. Distinguish an interest from an assumption about a job title. Include practical considerations such as study time and available support.'), ('Leave with a next action', 'Your fifth question is what you can do next to reduce uncertainty. It could be reading an official programme description, speaking with a practitioner or gathering application information. A useful guidance conversation turns uncertainty into a set of questions you can investigate.')]), ('opportunity-notes', 'Keep better notes when exploring school opportunities', 'Opportunity navigation', [('Record the original source', 'An opportunity listing is a starting point. Keep the institution name, original page address and the date you checked it. This lets you return to the source when a requirement changes or a summary leaves out an important detail.'), ('Separate requirements from assumptions', 'Create distinct notes for eligibility, required documents and questions still unanswered. Avoid treating a previous applicant’s experience as a current rule. If the institution has not confirmed something, mark it as unconfirmed.'), ('Build your own checklist', 'Use your notes to prepare a practical checklist for the route you are exploring. Review the checklist against the institution’s current instructions before acting. The SPSOW concept supports discovery; applicants still need to verify details with the responsible institution.')])]},
'wwi': {
'audience': [('Students balancing study and work', 'Work While Studying explores flexible workplace exposure that takes academic commitments seriously. CASMET applies this idea to supervised sales, marketing and business-development assignments.'), ('Graduates building experience', 'CAREER BRIDGE and JoSCA focus on connecting preparation, workplace understanding and the search for suitable experience. An enquiry begins a discussion about interests and readiness.'), ('Employers and learning partners', 'Help define meaningful assignments, supervision and feedback. A useful placement has a clear learning purpose alongside the work that needs to be completed.')],
'journey': [('Map readiness and availability', 'Describe your interests, relevant skills and available hours outside academic commitments. Identify any preparation or support needed before an assignment.'), ('Agree the assignment', 'Where a suitable opportunity is confirmed, clarify the task, supervisor, schedule, expectations and how work will be reviewed before participation begins.'), ('Capture experience and feedback', 'Keep an agreed record of work, learning and feedback. The proposed CASMET Skills Passport approach connects experience with evidence of developing capabilities.')],
'faq': [('Are CASMET and IVW open for immediate placement?', 'These are programme-design pathways in the GECP materials. Contact WWI to confirm current availability, employer arrangements and participation requirements.'), ('Can I choose hours around my studies?', 'Academic availability is an important part of the enquiry. Any schedule must be agreed with the programme and host organisation; submitting a form does not create a work commitment.')],
'blogs': [('casmet-learning', 'CASMET: connecting campus learning with practical enterprise', 'CASMET', [('A learning purpose for each assignment', 'CASMET stands for Campus Sales & Marketing Enterprise Team. Its programme concept connects student participation with supervised sales, marketing and business-development work. The useful question is not only what students will sell, but what they will learn from preparing, communicating and following through.'), ('Preparation before participation', 'The proposed Business Academy and Sales Ready components create space for preparation. Students need to understand the offer, the intended audience and the limits of their role. Academic availability, supervision and assignment expectations should be discussed before work begins.'), ('Record skills as well as outputs', 'A proposed Skills Passport can capture communication, planning, collaboration and reflection alongside completed tasks. Feedback gives the record meaning. This article explains the programme concept; it is not a recruitment announcement or a promise of earnings. Enquire with WWI about current delivery arrangements.')]), ('work-learning-record', 'How to turn workplace exposure into a learning record', 'Work experience', [('Describe the task and context', 'A useful record begins with what you were asked to do, the context and your responsibility. Avoid listing activities without explaining your contribution. Remove customer details, confidential information and material you do not have permission to share.'), ('Explain the learning', 'Describe a decision you made, a difficulty you encountered and the feedback that helped. Distinguish what you completed independently from what you completed with support. This makes the account credible and helps identify the next skill to develop.'), ('Connect reflection to the next step', 'End with one capability you want to practise further. Ask a supervisor which examples best represent your work and whether they can be included in a portfolio. WWI’s work-based learning approach is most useful when experience leads to reflection and a clearer development plan.')])]},
'rengtech': {
'audience': [('Technical learners', 'Explore the relationship between diagnosis, measurement, repair and documentation. Practical learning depends on suitable equipment, competent supervision and an agreed training setting.'), ('Asset owners and institutions', 'Discuss equipment restoration and productive reuse as a structured problem. Begin with the condition, intended use and available history of the asset.'), ('Makers and enterprise partners', 'Connect design thinking, fabrication and responsible reverse engineering with practical needs. Proposed hub activities include CAD, prototyping and documented engineering work.')],
'journey': [('Describe the technical challenge', 'Explain the asset or technical area, your experience and the outcome you want to explore. Do not attempt hazardous repairs to prepare an enquiry.'), ('Review feasibility and learning needs', 'The team can discuss the expertise, workspace, tools and assessment required. Workshop access and technical scope need separate confirmation.'), ('Document the process', 'Record observations, decisions, supervision and the limits of the work. Reuse decisions should be supported by appropriate technical assessment.')],
'faq': [('Is RENGTECH a separate foundation?', 'RENGTECH is described in the GECP material as an initiative connected with GCCS, with its own branded website.'), ('Can any donated equipment be refurbished?', 'Suitability depends on condition, intended use, parts, expertise and assessment. An initial enquiry does not establish that an asset is safe or economical to restore.')],
'blogs': [('repair-learning', 'Refurbishment as a pathway for technical learning', 'Practical engineering', [('Look beyond the broken component', 'Refurbishment can introduce learners to how parts, materials and systems work together. The educational value lies in understanding a problem and documenting a reasoned response, rather than simply making an object appear to work again.'), ('Make observation part of the process', 'An asset history, a condition record and a clear statement of intended use help frame the work. Technical tasks need suitable supervision and an appropriate workspace. Learning goals should be agreed alongside the boundaries of the practical activity.'), ('Treat documentation as an output', 'A useful record explains what was assessed, what changed and what remains uncertain. It supports discussion between learners and experienced practitioners. RENGTECH’s programme concept links technical capability with productive reuse; this article is an overview, not instructions for repairing equipment.')]), ('reverse-engineering-records', 'Why documentation matters in reverse engineering', 'Design thinking', [('Begin with a question', 'Reverse engineering starts with an enquiry into an existing design. A clear question helps decide what needs to be observed and recorded. The purpose might be understanding a mechanism, comparing a component or exploring a documented design improvement.'), ('Keep observations traceable', 'Separate direct observations from interpretations. Record how a measurement or sketch was produced and which limitations affect it. Where work involves third-party designs, agree the permitted scope and handling of material before beginning.'), ('Use the record to support discussion', 'Drawings, notes and prototypes become more useful when another practitioner can follow the reasoning. The proposed RENGTECH hub connects diagnosis, CAD and prototyping with learning. A traceable record helps mentors review the process and identify where further assessment is needed.')])]},
'spin': {
'audience': [('People with practical skills', 'Describe what you can contribute using examples of work, projects or community activity. SkillsPool connects capabilities with collaborative problem-solving.'), ('Project teams', 'Identify the different capabilities a challenge needs, including coordination, communication and implementation, as well as specialist technical skills.'), ('Mentors and collaborators', 'Support shared learning by reviewing work, asking useful questions and helping teams translate ideas into manageable experiments.')],
'journey': [('Describe your contribution', 'Share your strengths, a public work sample if available and the types of projects you would like to explore.'), ('Define a shared challenge', 'Clarify the problem, the intended users and the capabilities a team needs. Agree responsibilities before starting collaborative work.'), ('Build and review', 'Use small outputs and regular feedback to learn what works. Record both the result and the contribution of each participant.')],
'faq': [('Must I have a formal qualification to enquire?', 'The initial form asks about skills and project interests. Specific projects may have their own requirements, which should be confirmed with the team.'), ('Will my details appear in a public directory?', 'The enquiry form stores details for administrative review. It does not automatically publish a profile or portfolio.')],
'blogs': [('skills-evidence', 'Show a skill through evidence, not just a label', 'Skills development', [('Choose a concrete example', 'A broad label such as problem-solving means different things in different settings. Choose an example where you used the skill and explain the task. The example can come from learning, work, volunteering or a personal project.'), ('Make your contribution clear', 'State what you did, how you approached the challenge and what support you received. In a team project, distinguish your contribution from the whole team’s output. A short, honest explanation is more useful than a long list of claims.'), ('Identify a development edge', 'Explain what you would improve next time. This gives a potential collaborator a sense of both your capability and your willingness to learn. SPIN’s focus on connecting talent with projects benefits from descriptions grounded in practical evidence.')]), ('collaboration-brief', 'Write a clearer brief for a collaborative project', 'Collaboration', [('Name the problem', 'Start with the situation you want to improve and the people affected. Avoid defining the project only by a preferred tool. The team needs to understand the problem before deciding which skills and methods are appropriate.'), ('Describe a manageable first output', 'Choose something small enough to review: a sketch, a prototype, an interview summary or a process map. State what questions this first output should answer. This helps participants see how their contributions fit together.'), ('Agree how to work together', 'Discuss roles, availability, communication and how decisions will be recorded. Make room for different levels of experience and define how feedback will be handled. A clear brief creates a practical starting point for the shared problem-solving envisaged by SPIN.')])]},
'gsevs': {
'audience': [('Experienced and retired professionals', 'Contribute through mentoring, guest teaching, advisory work or knowledge documentation. Your availability and preferred service format help shape an appropriate conversation.'), ('Learners and early-career practitioners', 'Bring questions grounded in a learning or professional challenge. Mentoring works best when the participant has a purpose and space to reflect.'), ('Institutions and community organisations', 'Describe the capacity or expertise you need, the local context and how a volunteer contribution would be supported and followed through.')],
'journey': [('Map experience and interests', 'Introduce your professional background, areas of expertise and the kind of service you would find meaningful.'), ('Scope a contribution', 'Discuss the intended audience, task, availability and any preparation needed. Virtual and blended service are part of the programme concept.'), ('Share and preserve learning', 'Agree an output and a feedback process. With consent, lessons from an assignment can contribute to intergenerational knowledge transfer.')],
'faq': [('Do volunteers have to be retired?', 'The programme description includes both experienced and retired professionals. Enquire about the requirements for a particular contribution.'), ('Can volunteering be remote?', 'Virtual and digital expert service is one of the programme areas. A suitable format depends on the assignment, participants and available support.')],
'blogs': [('mentoring-conversation', 'Make a mentoring conversation useful to both generations', 'Mentoring', [('Begin with the learner’s question', 'Experience is most useful when it connects with a question the learner is trying to answer. Invite the learner to describe the situation before offering a solution. This helps the mentor understand the context rather than assume it matches a past experience.'), ('Share reasoning alongside experience', 'A story becomes a learning resource when it explains the choices, constraints and uncertainty involved. Describe what you considered and what you might do differently now. Leave space for the learner to identify which parts apply to their own situation.'), ('Agree a small next step', 'End with one action or reflection the learner can take forward. A follow-up conversation can explore what changed. GSEVS connects experience with mentoring, teaching and service; a useful exchange strengthens the learner’s judgement rather than replacing it.')]), ('expert-assignment', 'From broad expertise to a well-scoped volunteer assignment', 'Expert service', [('Understand the need', 'An organisation may ask for expertise without yet having a clear assignment. Discuss the problem, who experiences it and what support already exists. This helps distinguish a mentoring need from a teaching session, technical review or longer advisory engagement.'), ('Agree a realistic contribution', 'Define an output that fits the volunteer’s availability and the organisation’s capacity to participate. Clarify who will provide information, who will review the work and how questions will be resolved. Specific boundaries make a contribution easier to manage.'), ('Plan for continuity', 'Consider how the organisation will use the output after the assignment ends. A short handover, documented recommendations or a learning session can help. These questions support the GSEVS concept of service that transfers knowledge and strengthens local capability.')])]},
}
PROFILES.update({
'tradestart': {
'audience': [('Apprenticeship graduates', 'Explore the transition from technical practice to organising a small enterprise. Identify the tools, routines and customer relationships your next step would require.'), ('Tradespeople developing an enterprise', 'Bring questions about readiness, productive assets, mentoring and market connections. TRADESTART Green and Digital are proposed development pathways.'), ('Mentors and industry partners', 'Contribute practical business experience, sector knowledge or opportunities to understand customer needs. Support arrangements are discussed individually.')],
'journey': [('Describe your trade', 'Tell the team what you practise, your experience and the enterprise stage you are exploring.'), ('Identify the next capability', 'Separate technical, organisational and market questions. Prioritise one area where support could help you make progress.'), ('Discuss a support pathway', 'Explore relevant mentoring, training or productive-asset planning. Enquiries do not guarantee finance, equipment or a place on a programme.')],
'faq': [('Does TRADESTART automatically provide tools or funding?', 'No. Productive assets are an area of programme development. Specific support, eligibility and availability require confirmation.'), ('Can I enquire before starting a business?', 'Yes. Describe your trade and the stage you are at so the team can understand the preparation you want to explore.')],
'blogs': [('trade-enterprise', 'From knowing a trade to organising an enterprise', 'Enterprise readiness', [('Make the transition visible', 'Technical competence and enterprise organisation are related but different capabilities. A tradesperson may be confident in the work itself while still developing routines for enquiries, planning and customer communication. Recognising the difference makes it easier to ask for relevant support.'), ('Map a typical job', 'Describe the steps from a customer’s first question to an agreed handover. Identify where information can be lost or expectations left unclear. This exercise is a way to understand the work process, not a financial projection or a guarantee of demand.'), ('Choose one routine to improve', 'Start with a manageable improvement such as an enquiry checklist or an organised job record. Review it with a mentor who understands the trade. TRADESTART connects apprenticeship experience with enterprise preparation through practical learning and support conversations.')]), ('tools-purpose', 'Start with the work before making a tool wish list', 'Productive assets', [('Describe the task first', 'A productive-asset plan begins with the work an item is intended to support. Explain the task, the setting and the capability required. This gives a clearer basis for discussion than a list of equipment without context.'), ('Think beyond possession', 'An asset also needs suitable space, competent use, maintenance and a clear responsibility for its care. Write down what support would be needed to keep it useful. These questions are relevant whether equipment is owned, shared or accessed through a partner.'), ('Bring the plan to a discussion', 'Use the task description to ask focused questions of a mentor or programme team. TRADESTART’s productive-asset theme is about connecting tools with capability and enterprise readiness. This article does not announce a distribution scheme or imply that an enquiry will receive equipment.')])]},
'eraid': {
'audience': [('Schools and learning communities', 'Describe the learning activity that needs support and the gap in available resources. Clear needs help distinguish a useful contribution from an unsuitable donation.'), ('Resource contributors', 'Introduce learning materials or resources you can offer, including their condition, subject and intended audience. Distribution arrangements require discussion.'), ('Library and knowledge organisers', 'Explore ways to organise collections so learners can find, use and care for materials. Digital access includes usability and support, as well as availability.')],
'journey': [('Describe the learning need', 'Explain who will use the resource, for what purpose and in what setting. Use a broad town or region in the public form.'), ('Review suitability', 'Discuss content, condition, accessibility, format and practical arrangements before accepting or allocating resources.'), ('Plan access and stewardship', 'Identify how materials will be organised, made available and maintained. Feedback can help explain whether the resources meet the original need.')],
'faq': [('Can we request or offer materials?', 'The resource enquiry accepts both needs and offers. Include the type of resource and the organisation or community involved.'), ('Is every resource request fulfilled?', 'No. Allocation depends on suitability, availability and review. The form records an enquiry rather than a confirmed commitment.')],
'blogs': [('resource-need', 'Describe an educational resource need clearly', 'Learning resources', [('Begin with the learning activity', 'A request is easier to understand when it explains what learners are trying to do. Describe the subject, activity and setting before listing items. This connects the request with a purpose that potential contributors can assess.'), ('Explain the practical context', 'Note the intended audience, available storage or access arrangements and any relevant format needs. A resource that works in one setting may be difficult to use in another. Avoid including private learner information in an initial enquiry.'), ('Define useful support', 'State which part of the need matters most and what alternatives could help. ERAid’s resource-mobilisation concept benefits from a conversation about suitability. A clear request supports that conversation without assuming that every item can be supplied.')]), ('collection-use', 'A collection becomes valuable when people can use it', 'Libraries and access', [('Make resources findable', 'A shelf or digital folder is only the starting point. Simple categories, clear labels and an understandable access process help people discover what is available. Organise around the questions and activities of the intended users.'), ('Introduce the collection', 'Show learners how to locate a resource and where to ask for help. A short orientation can reveal confusing labels or missing information. Invite feedback from people using the collection for the first time.'), ('Keep learning from use', 'Review which materials are useful and which remain hard to access. Record condition and organisation issues so they can be addressed. ERAid connects educational resources with access; ongoing stewardship helps a collection continue serving its learning purpose.')])]},
'evm': {
'audience': [('Educators and knowledge holders', 'Share an idea or experience that could help others understand learning, research or career development. Start with the audience and the question your story addresses.'), ('Researchers communicating findings', 'Explore accessible explanations that retain the limits of the underlying evidence. The research-to-impact theme connects knowledge with practical public conversation.'), ('Media collaborators', 'Propose interviews, explainers or other formats, with attention to source verification, permissions and the context of participants’ contributions.')],
'journey': [('Pitch the story', 'Describe the subject, intended audience and suggested format. Include a public source link where available.'), ('Clarify evidence and permissions', 'Identify what can be supported, what remains uncertain and who needs to consent to participation or reuse.'), ('Shape an accessible explanation', 'Discuss language, structure and context. Publication decisions and production arrangements are confirmed separately.')],
'faq': [('Does submitting a story guarantee publication?', 'No. The enquiry starts an editorial conversation. Suitability, evidence, permissions and available production capacity need review.'), ('Can a proposal draw on research?', 'Yes. Include the public source and explain why it matters to the intended audience. Avoid presenting early or limited findings as settled conclusions.')],
'blogs': [('research-story', 'Turn a research idea into a story people can follow', 'Research communication', [('Start with the reader’s question', 'A research story needs a clear reason for someone outside the project to read it. Identify the question the work addresses and explain the context in familiar language. Avoid beginning with terminology that requires the reader to already know the field.'), ('Keep the evidence visible', 'Explain what was examined, what the work suggests and what it cannot establish. Link to the original source when it is public. An accessible account should help readers understand the evidence, not remove the uncertainty that gives it meaning.'), ('Connect without overstating', 'Describe possible relevance while distinguishing findings from proposed applications. EVM’s research-to-impact theme creates space for that conversation. A strong explanation gives readers enough context to ask their own questions about practical use.')]), ('voice-interview', 'Prepare an interview that gives knowledge a clearer voice', 'Educational voices', [('Agree the purpose', 'Explain the topic, audience and intended format before the conversation. Give the participant space to ask questions about how their contribution may be used. An interview works better when expectations are understood on both sides.'), ('Invite concrete experience', 'Ask for examples and follow up on unfamiliar terms. Listen for the reasoning behind a choice, not only the result. Avoid pushing a participant to speak beyond their experience or reveal information they should not share.'), ('Preserve the context', 'When shaping an account, keep the meaning of a contribution intact. Verify names and factual details and discuss any agreed review process. EVM’s educational storytelling can make knowledge accessible while respecting the person and context behind it.')])]},
'dricd': {
'audience': [('Teams defining a research question', 'Turn a broad information need into a focused brief with a purpose, scope and intended output. This provides a clearer basis for discovery and curation.'), ('Organisations managing knowledge', 'Explore ways to organise sources, documents and institutional information so that others can find and understand them.'), ('Researchers and communicators', 'Connect evidence synthesis with documentation and accessible outputs, while keeping the origin and limits of sources visible.')],
'journey': [('Frame the enquiry', 'Describe your topic, intended use, output and timeframe. A clear brief helps distinguish what is essential from what is outside scope.'), ('Organise the evidence', 'Discuss relevant source types, inclusion decisions and how notes and references should be recorded.'), ('Prepare a usable output', 'Agree the form of the synthesis or documentation and make unresolved questions explicit. Delivery scope and timelines require separate confirmation.')],
'faq': [('Is a public enquiry a commissioned research assignment?', 'No. It records your research interest and desired output. Scope, responsibilities and any delivery terms must be agreed separately.'), ('Can DRICD help organise existing documents?', 'Documentation and knowledge organisation are programme areas. Describe the collection at a high level; do not upload confidential material through the initial form.')],
'blogs': [('research-brief', 'Write a research brief that keeps the work focused', 'Research planning', [('Explain the decision or question', 'A useful brief says why the information is needed. Describe the question, the intended audience and how the output will be used. This helps distinguish a general exploration from a task that needs a specific kind of evidence.'), ('Set boundaries', 'State the topic boundaries, relevant period and the types of sources that may be useful. Identify what is outside the task. Boundaries can be refined as the work develops, but recording them makes changes visible rather than accidental.'), ('Agree an output', 'Specify whether the useful result is a source map, a synthesis, a briefing or an organised collection. Include a place for unanswered questions. DRICD connects discovery with practical documentation; a clear brief helps the output serve its intended purpose.')]), ('source-notes', 'Keep source notes that another person can follow', 'Information curation', [('Capture enough to return', 'Record the title, author or organisation, publication date where available and a stable source address. Add the date you accessed online material. These details help someone locate the source and understand which version you consulted.'), ('Separate quotation from interpretation', 'Keep direct quotations clearly marked and distinguish them from your summary. Add a note on why the source matters to the question. This reduces confusion when several sources use similar language or reach different conclusions.'), ('Record limits and gaps', 'Note missing context, uncertain dates or reasons a source may not fit the task. A collection of references becomes more useful when the selection and limitations are visible. Information curation is a process of organising judgement as well as organising documents.')])]},
'tkrd': {
'audience': [('Artisans and master practitioners', 'Explore ways to describe practice-based knowledge, judgement and techniques that may be difficult to capture in a conventional manual.'), ('Experienced professionals and retirees', 'Reflect on lessons, decisions and working methods that could help future learners, with control over what is shared and preserved.'), ('Communities and documentation partners', 'Shape consent-led knowledge capture around the knowledge holders, the intended audience and appropriate access arrangements.')],
'journey': [('Identify the knowledge and purpose', 'Describe the area of practice, the people involved and why documentation may be useful.'), ('Agree consent and boundaries', 'Discuss participation, recording, sensitive material, review and intended access before knowledge capture begins.'), ('Document and return for review', 'Combine context with examples and explanations. Agree how contributors can review the account and how approved material will be organised.')],
'faq': [('Does an enquiry transfer ownership of knowledge?', 'No. An initial enquiry is a conversation about possible documentation. Recording, reuse and access arrangements need explicit discussion and agreement.'), ('Must knowledge holders write a formal document?', 'The programme concept includes interviews, observation and oral-history approaches. The appropriate method depends on the knowledge holder and purpose.')],
'blogs': [('knowledge-questions', 'Ask about the judgement behind a practical skill', 'Tacit knowledge', [('Look beyond the visible steps', 'A skilled practitioner may make choices that are difficult to see from the outside. Ask what they notice, which signals matter and how they decide that something needs attention. These questions can reveal the judgement that sits behind a sequence of actions.'), ('Use a specific example', 'Invite the practitioner to describe an ordinary situation and a situation that required a different response. Comparing examples can help explain why a method changes with context. Leave room for uncertainty and for knowledge that is difficult to put into words.'), ('Check your understanding', 'Return your account to the knowledge holder and ask what it misses. A learner’s interpretation may flatten an important distinction. TKRD’s focus on practice-based knowledge makes review and context central to useful documentation.')]), ('consent-knowledge', 'Put consent at the centre of knowledge documentation', 'Knowledge preservation', [('Discuss the purpose before recording', 'Explain why documentation is proposed, who may use it and what form it may take. Knowledge holders should have space to ask questions and identify material they do not want included. An initial conversation is not the same as permission to publish.'), ('Agree a review process', 'Discuss how the contributor will see the account and how corrections or concerns will be handled. Record the agreed boundaries in a form participants understand. Where other people or community knowledge are involved, further discussion may be needed.'), ('Keep access deliberate', 'Preservation does not automatically mean unrestricted public access. Consider the intended users and the context needed to understand the material. TKRD connects knowledge capture with learning and transfer; thoughtful access arrangements help respect the people whose experience makes the record possible.')])]},
})
